# Gargoyle

## Lore

Gargoyles are huge demonic-looking creatures, giant lizard-like humanoids with massive bat wings, a sharp-toothed maw, dark green or grey-green skin and blazing yellow eyes. The reptilian appearance is deceptive: though they are hatched from eggs, they are warm-blooded. Their grim visage appears in legends throughout Earth''s history, which means they have visited this planet before.

Although considered demons, they are creatures from another dimension. They have a pack mentality and travel in vast flocks. In days past one rarely met more than a pair on Earth; since the coming of the rifts they are all too common, especially in Europe and China. Vast legions have joined forces with other supernatural and alien beings to create scattered kingdoms across Spain, France, Italy, Austria, Germany, Poland, Czechoslovakia, Hungary, Romania and Russia, and they war constantly against the New German Republic.

Gargoyles have a low intelligence and tend to rely on brute strength, natural powers and superior numbers - but they are not stupid, and they can be treacherous and are always ruthless. They are instinctively aggressive predators who work as individuals, in small groups, and as part of a large flock. They love to fight and will die with a toothy grin etched across their lips. Part of the pack mentality is to secure a territory, mate and expand, so their presence could spell disaster for Earth if the population is not held in check.

Gargoyles prefer heavy weapons - rail guns, pulse rifles, particle beam and plasma weapons - and both they and gurgoyles have a special fondness for vibro-blades, rune weapons and whips or chain weapons. Both are carnivores who feast on the flesh of any living creature that falls prey to them, from cattle to humans.

Males and females look nearly identical; females tend to be a little less muscular, have small horns and look a little softer. They are hatched from eggs and are not mammals, so females have no breasts, and none have nipples or a belly button.

## Playing a Gargoyle

Most gargoyles are anarchist or evil. Their anarchist society, predatory instincts and philosophy of "might makes right" leave little room for a good alignment to develop. Those who persist in being good are either reclusive individuals of the lowest social rank in the flock, or rogues who leave or are thrown out.

A gargoyle of good or anarchist alignment can join forces with humans and D-bees, but it will lead to hardship. Most Europeans loathe all gargoyles regardless of alignment or past good deeds, and many have adopted an attitude of shoot first and don''t bother with questions. Few Europeans other than gypsies and foreigners will ever trust a gargoyle; merchants will not sell to them, and people will spit in their faces rather than give them the time of day. Anyone who associates with them is regarded as the worst kind of scum. Asians share the sentiment. Gargoyles are far less feared and hated in North and South America, Canada and Africa, and in Atlantis, the New Phoenix Empire and other monster-dominated kingdoms they are welcomed and treated with respect.

Other gargoyles view a character who associates with humans, or who has a good alignment and a regard for all life, as weak, stupid and inferior. Evil and warlike gargoyles may call such a character a traitor and a coward, which leads to duels - and, if the character loses, to enslavement, imprisonment, torture or death.

## GM Notes

Gargoyles and gurgoyles are always loyal to their tribe or flock, even when that community has been adopted and is composed mostly of non-gargoyles. They work very well in a small team, and especially well under a gargoyle lord or a powerful supernatural being.

They do not get along with most true supernatural beings: they hate vampires, and detest ghouls and devilkins. The Gargoyle Empire has broken that rule and freely associates with most monster races other than vampires, which other gargoyles regard as a disturbing anomaly. They are especially friendly toward trolls and other giants, mutant animals and lesser demons, and many work for the Splugorth as guardians, scouts and slavers.

In most cases a gargoyle could never create, build or repair a device as simple as a flashlight - but they know how to use and maintain one, and they understand its function, value and danger well enough to respond appropriately. The Gargoyle Empire has added mega-damage weapons and armour to its arsenal: rail guns, laser rifles, vibro-blades, Kittani plasma weapons, explosives, the occasional vehicle, cybernetic implants, bionic limbs and weapons, M.O.M. conversions, power armour and robot vehicles.

As a slave in the gladiatorial arena a gargoyle commands 75,000 to 150,000 credits.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'gargoyle');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'gargoyle';

